Hold input back while a network grace period rides out a silent network. ENet queues every
reliable input packet while the host does not answer and delivers all of them, however old,
once the network returns; with a 20-60 second grace period that replays many seconds of stale
input and grows ENet's queue past the input stream's own bound. While a grace period is set and
nothing has come back from the host for a second (the 100 ms periodic ping is acked otherwise),
isControlContactLost() is true and the input send thread drops input before it is encrypted or
sequenced, keeping only the latest state of each key, mouse button and controller. When the
host is heard again (the control receive thread wakes the input thread, or the next input
packet notices), it sends that state, releases first, and cancels touches and pen contacts that
changed meanwhile. Mouse motion, scrolling and text from the silence are dropped. Packets
already handed to ENet before contact counted as lost are still delivered. Without a grace
period isControlContactLost() is always false and nothing changes.
During a grace period the control receive thread also stops spinning through a silence: ENet
does not ping while reliable data waits for an ack, so it waits for the resend timer instead of
waking at once for a ping that is never sent.
Applies after cursor-shape.patch.

